.TH DTDBUS 1 "October 2026" "CDE" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dtdbus \- the CDE session's freedesktop.org D-Bus services
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dtdbus
[\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-display\fR \fIdisplay\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dtdbus
answers the requests that today's applications make of the desktop
over D-Bus, for a CDE session.
.TP
.B org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver
(session bus).  A video player, or a browser that plays a video,
inhibits the screen saver: while any inhibition is held, the X
server's screen saver is suspended (and with it the monitor's power
saving), so the screen neither blanks nor locks.  An inhibition ends
when its application ends it or leaves the bus.
.B Lock
locks the display;
.B SimulateUserActivity
resets the idle timer;
.B GetSessionIdleTime
returns the idle time in seconds.
.TP
.B org.freedesktop.Notifications
(session bus), unless another notification service runs.
Notifications are shown at the top right of the screen until they
expire (6 seconds unless the application says otherwise; critical
ones stay) or are clicked.  A click invokes the notification's
default action, if it has one.  Markup is shown as plain text.
.TP
.B org.freedesktop.login1
(system bus).  When logind is asked to lock the session (for example
by
.BR "loginctl lock-session" ),
the display is locked; and before the machine goes to sleep,
.B dtdbus
locks the display, holding the suspend up (with a logind delay
inhibitor lock) until the session manager has answered, or 3 seconds,
so that the machine wakes up locked.
.PP
The display is locked through the session manager
.RB ( dtsession ),
with its ToolTalk request Display_Lock, as the LockDisplay action
does.
.PP
.B Xsession
starts
.B dtdbus
for each session; it exits when the session's X server connection
closes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
Report what is done (inhibitions, notifications, locking) on the
standard error, which Xsession sends to
.IR $HOME/.dt/startlog .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DTDBUS
Set to
.B no
in
.I $HOME/.dtprofile
to leave
.B dtdbus
out of the session.
.TP
.B XDG_SESSION_ID
The logind session to follow; without it, the session of the
process.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dtsession (1),
.BR Xsession (1),
.BR loginctl (1),
.BR systemd-inhibit (1)
